+\section{Security scans, policy and credentials (15 points)}\label{Section:deliveryScans}
+\textbf{Owner:} BD. \textbf{Status:} policy written before the first release; one real block and one synthetic block recorded \StatusPass; the instructor's acceptance of the dated exceptions is requested.
+
+\paragraph{Policy (written before the release).} The file \texttt{scan-policy.md} (course repository, \texttt{assignment-8/bd-work}) fixes what is scanned, when, and what blocks:
+\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{L{3.2cm}L{3.2cm}Y}
+\caption{Scan policy\label{Table:ScanPolicy}}\\
+\toprule \textbf{What} & \textbf{Tool} & \textbf{When and what blocks}\\ \midrule
+\endfirsthead
+\toprule \textbf{What} & \textbf{Tool} & \textbf{When and what blocks}\\ \midrule
+\endhead \bottomrule \endlastfoot
+Python dependencies & pip-audit 2.10.1 on the hashed lock & before the build; any known vulnerability, a scanner error, or a missing report blocks.\\
+Secrets in the source & Trivy 0.76.2, file-system secret scan & before the build; any finding blocks.\\
+Image & Trivy image scan, HIGH and CRITICAL & after the build, before the push; any finding not covered by a dated exception blocks.\\
+\end{tabularx}
+Fixes come first. A finding with a fixed version is fixed (bump the dependency or the base, rebuild, rescan). An unfixed finding needs a written exception with the identifier, the reason, an owner and an expiry no later than 14 days,
+reviewed in a pull request by the other person; an expired exception blocks again. There is no \texttt{|| true} anywhere in the pipeline.
+
+\paragraph{The gate on the real release.} Build \#6 passed tests, pip-audit and the secret scan, built the image, and then the image scan stopped it: 53 HIGH findings and no CRITICAL, 47 of them without any fixed version (\ListRef{213}).
+Nothing was pushed or deployed. Six findings had a fix and were fixed in the Dockerfile in pull request \#16 (the \texttt{zlib1g} security update, pinned to the exact version read from the tested image, and \texttt{pip}, \texttt{setuptools} and \texttt{wheel} removed
+after the locked install, which also removed the vendored urllib3, msgpack and setuptools findings). The 47 rows are 11 CVE identifiers in Debian base-image packages (util-linux, acl, ncurses, sqlite, systemd and perl); each is listed in \texttt{.trivyignore.yaml}
+with a reason in \texttt{security/exceptions.md}. The base-image findings are about local privilege escalation, mount helpers and libraries the service does not call; the service runs as UID 10001 on a read-only file system with all capabilities dropped.
+\textbf{The exceptions expire on 2026-11-30 at 00:00~UTC, that is Sunday, November 29, 2026, 7~pm in New York}; after that every build blocks again until a reviewed pull request renews or removes them. The instructor's acceptance is requested for the use of these exceptions.
+
+\paragraph{What reached the registry.} The package page of the \texttt{pricing} image lists exactly three versions: the commits \texttt{975d13c} (v1, build \#7), \texttt{102da1d} (build \#8, staged and not promoted) and \texttt{06550eb} (v2, build \#11). The image of the blocked build \#6 and nothing of the fixture build \#12 was ever pushed, which is what the order scan, then push, guarantees The page of the v2 version shows the digest the pipeline recorded and the user \texttt{jenkins-bot} as publisher (\FigRef{242}).
+
+\paragraph{After the fix.} Every later build keeps the full report (\texttt{trivy-image-full.json}: 47 HIGH rows) and gates on the filtered one (0 rows). The tested change: the image built from the modified Dockerfile scans with 0 findings and the service
+answered \texttt{/health} and \texttt{/price}; the pinned package installed in the real build (\ListRef{213}, \ListRef{216}).
+
+\paragraph{Blocked fixture.} To show each scanner block a bad change without putting anything into the repository, BD replayed build \#11 with a fixture step: a temporary directory with a known-vulnerable pin (urllib3 1.25.8) and a fake credential in the shape of a GitHub token
+(assembled at run time, never valid). Result (\ListRef{239}): pip-audit exit 1 with 21 vulnerabilities, Trivy secret scan exit 1 with one CRITICAL finding of rule \texttt{github-pat}, the line \texttt{BLOCKED}, and every later stage (image build, push, staging, approval, production)
+skipped; no image was built, nothing was pushed, both environments were untouched, and the temporary directory was removed.
+
+\paragraph{Credentials and access.} Nothing secret is in Git, in the chat with the assistant, in command arguments, or in logs; BD typed or pasted each secret herself.
+\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{L{3.2cm}L{3.4cm}Y}
+\caption{Credentials of the delivery pipeline\label{Table:DeliveryCredentials}}\\
+\toprule \textbf{Credential} & \textbf{Where it lives} & \textbf{Scope and risk}\\ \midrule
+\endfirsthead
+\toprule \textbf{Credential} & \textbf{Where it lives} & \textbf{Scope and risk}\\ \midrule
+\endhead \bottomrule \endlastfoot
+\texttt{deploy-ssh-key} & Jenkins credential only (\FigRef{204}); the local key files were deleted & ed25519 key for the \texttt{deploy} account: key-only login, no password, passwordless \texttt{sudo} (documented as host-administrator power, \ListRef{203}); revoke by deleting the line in \texttt{authorized\_keys}.\\
+\texttt{gitea-registry-write} & Jenkins credential, bound only in the push stage & Gitea user \texttt{jenkins-bot}, package scope only, with an expiry (\FigRef{207}); the account has more rights in the \texttt{developers} team (\FigRef{209}), so the token is narrower than the account.\\
+registry read token & root's \texttt{/root/.docker/config.json} on the host & read-only, with an expiry; stored in plain text, readable by root and so by the \texttt{deploy} account and the agent; used for the pull by digest (\ListRef{211}).\\
+Jenkins users & Jenkins' own database & exactly \texttt{brahmi} and \texttt{sakshi} may approve (\FigRef{210}).\\
+\end{tabularx}
+The deploy path is the host's Docker bridge (alias \texttt{deploy-target}) with a pinned SSH host key, because the host cannot reach its own Elastic IP on port 22 through the security group (\ListRef{201}). The two public hostnames, \texttt{staging} and \texttt{app}, are served by Caddy with
+Let's Encrypt certificates issued within seconds of the reload (\ListRef{208}); both are public on purpose because the service is stateless and Ansible checks the public name.
